 go  out without any uh danger that there is  not unanimity  paul volcker the senior american  diplomat who i'm pretty sure you will  know said last week that that  program that you've just outlined would  be the right thing to do but even so he  did not think  that it would prevent  a russian invasion because if that's  what mr putin wants to do that's what he  wants to do it's what he will do and  nothing we can do can stop him  well you know we know that president  putin wants to declare a sphere of  influence that extends way beyond the  borders of russia itself we know you he  would prefer to have a different  government in ukraine one that is  in his own hands although i'm not sure  about all this information this morning  about uh the particular names  they don't seem to add up  uh but  the important thing is that he i i've  met him on a number occasions i've dealt  with him i've had to stand up to him  when we had the assassination on british  soil  but he's an opportunist he probably does  not himself know what he's going to do  next we know what he wants to do the  question is what he will do the  important thing is at this particular  point in time and there's going to be  discussions the defense secretary is  going to moscow there's letters  exchanging between america and the um  the russians at this point we've got to  show strength and that's i think what  was  uh something of  an issue in the last few days when it  wasn't clear what the germans were doing  the french were talking about a separate  or appear to be talking about a separate  deal that was a european deal with  russia and of course uh there is this  issue about energy prices and what is  actually going to happen if the flow of  gas continues to slow to the west uh all  these issues are going to be taken into  account but the important thing you said  got to have a show of strength you you  said you weren't that sure about the uh  intelligence which has been published  today do you think t