y  that we're going to have a massive  uh review of all rules governing uh  public life in this country because  clearly you cannot run downing street  the way it's been run  you cannot make appointments in the way  they've been made  and you cannot allow the sort of  behavior that is that has happened and  hope somehow the public will not notice  when you're asking them to take  decisions that you're not prepared to  take  themselves look sue grey has got a  report on the the present uh issues  all right you're quite you're quite  right to say let's let's focus on the  things that matter and if i before i let  you go can i just ask you one specific  thing which probably you would know  about better than anybody else in the  world you were the last person to hike  national insurance do you think that  chancellor should go ahead with that or  should it be delayed as  many people are suggesting  well i'm not convinced that national  insurance is the right uh tax to deal  with the problems that we're facing  social care in particular  and i think kyosama is right you've got  to look at people's standards of living  as a whole and what costs they're going  to have to bear  i'm a patron of a charity in fife and  we're dealing with poverty that i have  not seen in my lifetime  we're dealing with people who can't heat  their homes we're dealing with children  who are not going to school well clad  and going to school hungry  and we're dealing with a crisis among so  many people because they are demoralized  and facing mental health problems as a  result of what has happened as a result  of this crisis now to impose upon those  families  an energy price rise in the heating  bills and in their electricity  then to impose a tax rise for those who  are paying  national insurance  at a time when food prices are also  rising at the same time and you've had  this 20 pounds cut in people's universal  credit it is simply not acceptable  and the the devastating effect on  poverty it